import{_ as o,o as t,c as d,a5 as a}from"./chunks/framework.DSg0KOwT.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"State and History Server intake","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"contributor-guide/architecture/state-and-history.md","filePath":"contributor-guide/architecture/state-and-history.md"}'),r={name:"contributor-guide/architecture/state-and-history.md"};function s(n,e,c,i,l,h){return t(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="state-and-history-server-intake" tabindex="-1">State and History Server intake <a class="header-anchor" href="#state-and-history-server-intake" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;State and History Server intake&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="state-model" tabindex="-1">State model <a class="header-anchor" href="#state-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;State model&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>src/store/store.ts</code> is a single Zustand store (<code>createStore</code> from <code>zustand/vanilla</code>, wrapped by a <code>useStore</code> hook). It holds all shared state; no component keeps a <code>useState</code> of its own for anything shared. Run state is one value: <code>run: AnalyzedRun | null</code> and its <code>runKey</code>, plus <code>taskDataCache</code> (the per-task detail fetched so far). The <code>AnalyzedRun</code> (<code>packages/core/src/analyzed-run.ts</code>) holds the parsed <code>appModel</code> (with the evidence ledger, <code>skippedLines</code> and <code>unreadableSqlExecutions</code>), the detector <code>catalog</code>, the <code>configFindings</code>, the <code>interpretation</code> (see <a href="#run-interpretation">Run interpretation</a>) and a <code>taskData</code> source. Views read it through <code>selectAppModel</code>, <code>selectCatalog</code> and <code>selectConfigFindings</code>, or <code>run.interpretation</code> through <code>src/view/interpretation.ts</code>. File and parse state: <code>activeFileId</code>, <code>sessionCache</code> (in-memory snapshots, each a <code>SerializedRun</code>, for instant file-switching), <code>parse: {pct, lines, etaMs}</code>, <code>status: &#39;idle&#39;|&#39;parsing&#39;|&#39;ready&#39;|&#39;error&#39;</code>, <code>shsParsing</code>, <code>errorMessage</code>, <code>errorNonce</code>. View state: <code>theme</code>, <code>widgetDensity</code>, <code>comparison</code>/<code>compareLoad</code>/<code>compareSeed</code>, <code>planGraph</code>. Export-only: <code>exportMode</code> and <code>exportProvenance</code>, both set by <code>src/export/hydrate-store.ts</code>.</p><p>A run enters the store one way: <code>installRun(run, runKey)</code>. It replaces <code>run</code> and <code>runKey</code> whole, sets <code>status</code> to <code>ready</code> and starts <code>taskDataCache</code> from the run&#39;s task data. There is no setter for a single run field. <code>runKey</code> is the file id, else a generated <code>run-N</code> for a folder or History Server load; <code>App.tsx</code> renders <code>&lt;DashboardRoute key={runKey} /&gt;</code>, so a new run remounts the dashboard and every piece of per-run view state (page, sort, open cards, the core-usage histogram) starts fresh. A view does not reset that state from an effect on <code>appModel</code> or <code>activeFileId</code>; the ast-grep rule <code>no-run-reset-effect</code> flags one.</p><p>A run&#39;s <code>taskData</code> says where its per-task detail comes from. A live parse holds <code>{ kind: &#39;worker&#39;, request }</code>, and <code>getTaskData</code> asks the worker for a stage the cache lacks. A restored or drilled-into run has no worker: it holds <code>{ kind: &#39;snapshot&#39;, stages }</code>, the stages prefetched before it left the screen, or <code>{ kind: &#39;none&#39; }</code>. A run read from an export is always <code>none</code>. The stage dialog, the core-usage histogram and the task spread read it through <code>useLiveTaskData</code> and say &quot;isn&#39;t available for this run&quot; instead of fetching.</p><p><code>src/store/useIngest.ts</code> is the only writer during a parse. Each load builds into a fresh <code>AppModel</code> it owns. On <code>done</code> it calls <code>analyzeRun</code> (the one place the detectors, the config audit, the evidence ledger and the interpretation run) and installs the result. Components never talk to the worker: they use <code>useIngest()</code>&#39;s returned actions (<code>startLoad</code>/<code>startLoadFolder</code>/<code>startLoadFromUrl</code>/<code>pickRecent</code>/<code>getTaskData</code>/ <code>resetToDropZone</code>/<code>cancelParse</code>, plus the comparison actions <code>prepareComparison</code>/<code>startCompareLoad</code>/<code>compareWithAnotherRun</code>/<code>drillIntoRun</code>) and the store&#39;s read state. <code>resetToDropZone</code> snapshots the current run into <code>sessionCache</code> with <code>serializeRun</code> before resetting; <code>pickRecent</code> and <code>drillIntoRun</code> install <code>deserializeRun(snapshot)</code>. <code>cancelParse</code> (the parse screen&#39;s <strong>Cancel</strong>) terminates the worker and never snapshots, so a half-parsed model can&#39;t be restored later.</p><p>Every parse ends in <code>ready</code> or <code>error</code>, never in a stuck <code>parsing</code>. A throw in <code>runDone</code> before the run is ready (analysis on odd log data) and a throw while opening a compare operand set <code>errorMessage</code>; once the run is <code>ready</code>, a failed task-data prefetch or recent-files write is logged and the dashboard stays.</p><p>Render errors stay local. <code>ErrorBoundary</code> (<code>src/view/ErrorBoundary.tsx</code>) wraps each grid card in <code>WidgetGridItem</code>, so a widget that throws on a log&#39;s data shows an error card with <strong>Retry</strong> while its siblings keep rendering. Picking another recent run remounts the dashboard, so the new run never shows the previous run&#39;s error. <code>AppErrorBoundary</code> wraps the app routes in <code>src/App.tsx</code>: a throw outside a card, or a lazy chunk that fails to load, shows a page with <strong>Reload page</strong> and <strong>Load another log</strong> (which runs <code>cancelParse</code>).</p><p><code>resetModel()</code> clears <code>run</code>, <code>runKey</code> and <code>taskDataCache</code>, returns <code>status</code> to idle, clears <code>shsParsing</code> and <code>errorMessage</code>, and closes any comparison or plan-graph view. It runs on every new parse, reset-to-drop-zone, cancelled parse, typed SHS failure and compare-load parse. The plan-graph model memo cache is keyed by the run&#39;s <code>appModel</code>, so a new run never reads a previous run&#39;s models (see <a href="./drill-down/plan-graph.html#plan-graph-view">Plan graph view</a>).</p><h3 id="serializing-a-run" tabindex="-1">Serializing a run <a class="header-anchor" href="#serializing-a-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Serializing a run&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>serializeRun</code> and <code>deserializeRun</code> (<code>packages/core/src/run-serialization.ts</code>) are the one pair that turns an <code>AnalyzedRun</code> into plain data and back, for the session snapshots in <code>sessionCache</code> and for the HTML export. <code>SerializedRun</code> lists every field with its collections as arrays, and the <code>FIELDS</code> table gives each one the run or model field it carries and its redaction policy: <code>redact</code> (pseudonymized), <code>keep</code> (counts and ids), <code>derive</code> (recomputed from the redacted run) or <code>drop</code>. <code>FIELDS</code> and the <code>SerializedRun</code> type are checked against <code>AnalyzedRun</code> and <code>AppModel</code>, so a new field does not compile until it is serialized and given a policy, and <code>redactRun</code> (<code>packages/core/src/redact.ts</code>) follows the table instead of listing fields. <code>packages/core/test/run-serialization.test.js</code> round-trips every fixture log and fails when a run or model field has no entry.</p><h3 id="run-interpretation" tabindex="-1">Run interpretation <a class="header-anchor" href="#run-interpretation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run interpretation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The rule: whatever states a conclusion about this run is computed once by the producer, whatever responds to what the viewer does is computed live.</p><p><code>interpretRun</code> (<code>packages/core/src/run-interpretation.ts</code>) is that one computation, and <code>analyzeRun</code> calls it, so the interpretation is a field of the run; its <code>RunInterpretation</code> interface lists what it returns (verdict and next steps, which checks could not run and why, formatted savings and their rank, the Scorecard figures and flags, per-stage finding order, the Findings board&#39;s rollup, and per finding type the widget order, doc anchor and clean-check criterion). It refers to findings by their <code>id</code>, which is unique across <code>catalog</code> and <code>configFindings</code>, so the order the findings are held in never matters. <code>src/view/interpretation.ts</code> resolves the ids to the finding objects the run holds, so reference-identity routing keeps working.</p><p>Widgets read the interpretation through <code>src/view/interpretation.ts</code>. What stays live responds to the viewer: filtering, sorting and expanding; the Findings board regrouped over the findings a filter keeps (<code>boardRollup</code>: unfiltered, it renders the carried groups as is; a time group&#39;s recoverable figure is capped by the union of its members&#39; stage intervals, so a filtered subset cannot be re-totalled from the carried per-group numbers); and the Scaling Simulator&#39;s <code>simulateScaling</code>, a what-if the viewer drives. A widget test that renders one widget with findings as props installs a run first with <code>tests/view/_shared/interpretation.ts</code> or <code>tests/view/_shared/run.ts</code>.</p><p>Some view judgments sit outside the rule. PlanView&#39;s cross-join, long-filter and exchange-count warnings and the plan graph&#39;s duration-share heat colours use thresholds in the view code, with no finding behind them. Do not copy that pattern. StageTable follows the rule: a Retried or Spill cell gets a chip only when the <code>retryWaste</code> or <code>spill</code> detector flagged the stage, in that finding&#39;s impact band, and Fetch Wait and I/O Ratio show raw numbers.</p><p>The HTML export carries the same result. <code>buildHtmlExportData</code> (<code>packages/core/src/html-export.ts</code>), shared by the CLI&#39;s <code>--export-html</code> and the dashboard&#39;s download, takes the <code>AnalyzedRun</code> and writes it as an <code>ExportRunData</code> (<code>SerializedRun</code> plus provenance, <code>EXPORT_DATA_SCHEMA_VERSION</code> 4; <code>taskData</code> is always null) with a provenance stamp: core version, core build id (<code>coreSourceHash</code> of the core sources: <code>coreBuildId</code> in <code>load-vendored.js</code> for the CLI, a Vite <code>define</code> for the web build, see <code>src/build-info.ts</code>) and producer, all shown in the export footer. With redaction on, <code>redactRun</code> redacts the run model and findings first and derives the interpretation from the redacted copy, so no text derived from a redacted value (a quoted failure reason cut mid-host, say) keeps a fragment of it; the md/json report does the same. The export bundle never analyzes a run: <code>hydrateExportStore</code> installs the payload&#39;s run with <code>installRun</code>, interpretation as is. <code>src/export/main-export.tsx</code> refuses, before rendering, any payload whose <code>schemaVersion</code> it was not built for, or that lacks its <code>configFindings</code>, <code>interpretation</code>, <code>stages</code>, <code>sql</code> or <code>jobs</code> (<code>unsupportedPayloadReason</code>). A missing or damaged <code>data.js</code> (no payload global, truncated gzip, invalid JSON) gets the same view with a &quot;missing or unreadable&quot; reason: <code>loadExportPayload</code> never throws. An exported file therefore shows the conclusions of the core that wrote it, and cannot be reinterpreted by a later bundle.</p><p>What the bundle may import is checked at build time. <code>vite.export.config.ts</code> resolves the live-only modules to stand-ins in <code>src/export/live-only-stubs/</code> (<code>useIngest</code>, <code>useRecentFiles</code>, <code>EvidenceExport</code>, <code>core-usage-histogram-data</code>), whose controls the exported dashboard hides or replaces with an &quot;isn&#39;t available for this run&quot; note. Then <code>exportAnalysisGuard</code> (<code>scripts/export-analysis-guard.mjs</code>) fails the build if the graph from <code>src/export/main-export.tsx</code> reaches a <code>packages/core/</code> module missing from <code>ALLOWED_EXPORT_CORE_MODULES</code>, or any of <code>LIVE_ONLY_MODULES</code>, naming each module and one importer chain back to the entry. <code>tests/export-analysis-guard.test.js</code> runs the real export build (<code>--mode guard</code>) so <code>npm test</code> catches a regression. A new core import off the allowlist fails the build: move its conclusion into <code>interpretRun</code>, or, if it only responds to what the viewer does, add it to the allowlist. The guard checks modules, not code: a threshold written straight into a widget passes it. The detector docs pages reuse this graph: <code>npm run build:docs-widget</code> (<code>--mode docs-widget</code>) builds the example app <code>docs-widget.html</code>, with the same stand-ins and guard, as multi-file output under <code>docs-site/public/</code>.</p><h3 id="finding-filter-state" tabindex="-1">Finding filter state <a class="header-anchor" href="#finding-filter-state" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Finding filter state&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The board-wide finding filter (impact band, raw <code>finding.type</code>, stage) lives outside the Zustand store, in <code>FindingFilterContext</code> (<code>src/view/FindingFilterContext.tsx</code>): a <code>createContext</code>+<code>useState</code><code>FilterSelection</code> (<code>src/view/finding-filter.ts</code>, three <code>Set</code>s) that every widget filters <code>catalog</code> through via <code>filterFindings</code>. It is seeded from the URL&#39;s <code>impact</code>/<code>type</code>/<code>stage</code> query params on mount and nowhere else, so a reload with no params gives the default board. A selection that names no impact band lists critical and warning findings only (<code>matchesFilter</code>); info findings show once the selection names the info band. Every change writes those params back with <code>history.replaceState</code>, never <code>push</code>, so filtering doesn&#39;t grow Back history. A <code>popstate</code> listener re-seeds the selection from the URL, so Back and forward re-apply filters.</p><p>Switching runs resets the selection to empty: the provider sits inside the <code>runKey</code>-keyed <code>DashboardRoute</code>, and its unmount strips the filter params so the remount seeds a clean slate. A page reload re-reads the address bar, so deep-linked filtered URLs still restore. Filters are seeded only from the URL and never persisted anywhere else (no localStorage, no restore-as-default): a filtered view silently becoming the default on reload would risk hiding findings from a user who didn&#39;t realize a filter was still active, so a plain reload with no filter params is always the default board.</p><h3 id="board-state" tabindex="-1">Board state <a class="header-anchor" href="#board-state" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Board state&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Findings board&#39;s state for the loaded run is the store&#39;s <code>board</code> slice (<code>src/store/board.ts</code>), replaced whenever a run loads (<code>resetModel</code> and a change of the active file). It holds the active tab, the set of open cards, the cards a route has revealed, the expanded group, the active route and the line holding focus. It also holds what widgets remember while their body is unmounted, by key: a page, a sort, the Stage Summary&#39;s filter and sorting, the Clean checks disclosure (<code>useWidgetState</code>, <code>src/view/useWidgetState.ts</code>). What the board lists is not in the store: it is the pure layout <code>buildBoardLayout</code> computes from the interpretation, the finding filter and the density, and routes resolve against it (see <a href="./widget-rendering/investigation-routing.html">First investigation routing</a>).</p><h3 id="recent-files-vs-session-cache" tabindex="-1">Recent files vs. session cache <a class="header-anchor" href="#recent-files-vs-session-cache" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recent files vs. session cache&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Two mechanisms cover reopening a file, at different lifetimes.</p><p><code>sessionCache</code> (in the Zustand store, see <a href="#state-model">State model</a>) is in-memory and per-session: it makes switching between files already loaded in the current tab instant, and it is gone on reload.</p><p>Recent files (<code>packages/core/src/recent-files.ts</code>, consumed by <code>src/view/useRecentFiles.ts</code>, <code>DropZone.tsx</code> and <code>useIngest.ts</code>) is IndexedDB-backed and cross-session. It persists each file&#39;s <code>FileSystemFileHandle</code> plus light metadata (name, size, <code>lastModified</code>, app name, issue count, <code>lastOpenedAt</code>), capped at 10 entries with the oldest evicted past the cap, so a file can be reopened after a full browser restart, pending the browser re-granting permission on the handle. Picking a recent entry re-parses from the handle; no parsed model is ever persisted.</p>',27)])])}const g=o(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
